---
// The front page: what barbican does, next to its UI, why use it, its parts, how they fit, how to install it, what
// changed recently, and where to read on.
import { Code, Tabs, TabItem } from '@astrojs/starlight/components';
import Screenshot from './Screenshot.astro';
import { releases } from '../changelog';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const link = (path: string) => `${base}${path}`;
const download = 'https://github.com/nikarh/barbican/releases/latest/download';

// What barbican is made of, each with what exists today.
const parts = [
	[
		'Log inputs',
		'A container’s log through the Docker API, a file followed across rotation, the systemd journal, or a VictoriaLogs query for logs shipped from other machines.',
		'/guides/sources/#inputs',
	],
	[
		'Parsers',
		'Traefik access logs, OpenSSH, Authelia, and a regular expression for anything else. Any parser works with any input.',
		'/guides/sources/#parsers',
	],
	[
		'Rules',
		'Match paths, methods, statuses, hosts, user agents, user names and lists, and ban once a threshold is reached within a time window. The defaults catch exploit probes, scanners and password guessing.',
		'/guides/rules/',
	],
	[
		'Lists',
		'Countries and continents from a geolocation database, and feeds such as Spamhaus DROP, FireHOL level 1, blocklist.de, Tor exits, or Cloudflare’s ranges to allow.',
		'/guides/feeds/',
	],
	[
		'Firewalls',
		'nftables on the server and on OpenWrt; iptables with ipset on router firmware without nftables. Blocks are kernel sets with timeouts, so they hold while barbican is stopped.',
		'/internals/firewall/',
	],
	[
		'Packages',
		'Debian and Arch packages and static binaries for x86_64 and aarch64; OpenWrt packages with LuCI pages for armv7, aarch64 and x86_64; the UI as a container image.',
		'/install/overview/',
	],
];
---

<div class="home not-content">
	<section class="intro">
		<div class="lead">
			<p class="summary">
				barbican reads your servers’ logs, decides with rules which addresses are abusing your services, and blocks
				them in the kernel, on the server and on your router.
			</p>
			<p>
				It is one program with one configuration file and a web UI. Countries and public blocklists can be blocked as a
				whole, people who log in are never locked out, and every ban says which rule made it and what the address did.
			</p>
			<div class="actions">
				<a class="button primary" href={link('/start/quick-start/')}>Quick start</a>
				<a class="button" href={link('/start/introduction/')}>Read the docs</a>
				<a class="button" href="https://github.com/nikarh/barbican">Source on GitHub</a>
			</div>
			<p class="meta">Linux with nftables; OpenWrt and iptables routers. MIT or Apache-2.0. Written in Rust.</p>
		</div>
		<div class="shot">
			<Screenshot
				name="overview"
				alt="barbican's overview: bans and events of the last 30 days, recent bans, the firewall, feeds, sources and edges, bans by country and events by rule."
			/>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="why">
		<h2>Why barbican</h2>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Everything in one place.</strong> Bans from logs, country blocking and blocklists usually take three tools
				and a dashboard. Here they share one set of rules, one allow list and one UI.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Blocks where traffic arrives.</strong> If your server sits behind a proxy or a router’s port forward, a ban
				on the server alone may not stop anything. Edge agents apply the same blocks on the router.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Small enough for a router.</strong> Lists are merged into ranges before they reach the kernel, the edge agent
				uses 6 to 10 MB, and it writes to flash only when it stops.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Hard to turn against you.</strong> The UI has no network port of its own, edges trust only your agent’s
				certificate, and logins make an address trusted.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p><a href={link('/start/comparison/')}>Compared with fail2ban, CrowdSec and others</a></p>
	</section>

	<section class="covers">
		<h2>What it is made of</h2>
		<p class="covers-lead">
			Each part has an interface of its own, and these are the implementations that exist today.
		</p>
		<dl>
			{
				parts.map(([term, text, href]) => (
					<div>
						<dt>
							<a href={link(href!)}>{term}</a>
						</dt>
						<dd>{text}</dd>
					</div>
				))
			}
		</dl>
	</section>

	<section class="flow">
		<h2>How the pieces fit</h2>
		<div class="flow-grid">
			<pre
				class="diagram"
				role="img"
				aria-label="Logs from each server reach the agent, which parses them, applies the rules, records to SQLite and blocks in the server's kernel; over TLS it pushes the blocks to edge agents on routers, which block in theirs.">{` server logs ─► agent: parse ─► rules ─► bans, trust ─► SQLite
 (local, or shipped        │
  from other servers)      ├─► kernel firewall on this server
                           └─► TLS ─► edge agents ─► kernel firewall on routers`}</pre>
			<p>
				One <strong>agent</strong> holds the rules, the lists and the history, and blocks on its own server. Logs from other
				servers can be shipped to it. <strong>Edge agents</strong> on routers connect to it and block what it blocks, so an
				address banned for probing one service can’t reach any of them. The <strong>UI</strong> talks to the agent through
				a local socket, and reaches browsers only through your authenticating reverse proxy.
			</p>
			<p>
				<a href={link('/guides/deployment/')}>Planning a deployment</a> ·{' '}
				<a href={link('/internals/how-it-works/')}>How a log line becomes a ban</a>
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="phone">
		<div class="phone-text">
			<h2>On a phone</h2>
			<p>
				The UI is served by a small container next to your reverse proxy, behind its login. It works under a path prefix,
				and its layout follows the screen: on a phone the panels stack, the grids keep the columns that matter, and the
				tabs move to the bottom.
			</p>
			<p><a href={link('/guides/reverse-proxy/')}>Behind a reverse proxy</a> · <a href={link('/guides/ui/')}>The web UI</a></p>
		</div>
		<div class="phones">
			<Screenshot name="overview-phone" alt="The overview on a phone." />
			<Screenshot name="bans-phone" alt="The bans on a phone." />
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="install">
		<h2>Install</h2>
		<p>
			Static binaries and packages for x86_64 and aarch64, edge agents for OpenWrt and for routers without nftables.
			Release files are signed. Details are in <a href={link('/install/overview/')}>Install</a>.
		</p>
		<Tabs syncKey="distro">
			<TabItem label="Debian / Ubuntu">
				<Code
					lang="bash"
					code={`arch=$(dpkg --print-architecture)    # amd64 or arm64
curl -fLO "${download}/barbican_\${arch}.deb"
sudo apt install "./barbican_\${arch}.deb"`}
				/>
			</TabItem>
			<TabItem label="Arch Linux">
				<Code
					lang="bash"
					code={`curl -fLO "${download}/barbican-$(uname -m).pkg.tar.zst"
sudo pacman -U "barbican-$(uname -m).pkg.tar.zst"`}
				/>
			</TabItem>
			<TabItem label="OpenWrt">
				<Code
					lang="bash"
					code={`cd /tmp    # the edge agent and its LuCI pages; armv7, aarch64 or x86_64
wget ${download}/barbican-edge-armv7.apk
wget ${download}/luci-app-barbican.apk
apk add --allow-untrusted ./barbican-edge-armv7.apk ./luci-app-barbican.apk`}
				/>
			</TabItem>
		</Tabs>
		<p class="after">
			Then say where the logs are, and start it in dry run first: <code>[firewall] enabled = false</code> decides and
			records everything, and blocks nothing.
		</p>
	</section>

	{
		releases.length > 0 && (
			<section class="changes">
				<div class="changes-head">
					<h2>Recent releases</h2>
					<a href={link('/reference/changelog/')}>All releases</a>
				</div>
				{/* Versions only: a release's full list of changes belongs on the changelog page, not on the front page. */}
				<ul class="release-list">
					{releases.slice(0, 3).map((r) => (
						<li>
							<a href={r.url}><code>v{r.version}</code></a>
							{r.date && <span class="release-date">{r.date}</span>}
							<a class="release-count" href={link('/reference/changelog/')}>what changed</a>
						</li>
					))}
				</ul>
			</section>
		)
	}

	<section class="next">
		<h2>Reading order</h2>
		<ol>
			<li><a href={link('/start/introduction/')}>Introduction</a>: the parts, and what barbican isn’t.</li>
			<li><a href={link('/guides/deployment/')}>Planning a deployment</a>: where each part runs so that blocks work.</li>
			<li><a href={link('/start/quick-start/')}>Quick start</a>: one server, one log, a dry run, then blocking.</li>
			<li><a href={link('/guides/sources/')}>Sources</a> and <a href={link('/guides/rules/')}>rules</a>: what is read, and what counts as abuse.</li>
			<li><a href={link('/edge/overview/')}>Edge agents</a>: blocking for the whole network on a router.</li>
			<li><a href={link('/reference/configuration/')}>Configuration</a>: every setting.</li>
			<li><a href={link('/internals/how-it-works/')}>How it works</a>: the engine, the firewall, the edge link and the UI’s protection.</li>
		</ol>
	</section>
</div>

<style>
	.release-list {
		list-style: none;
		padding: 0;
		margin: 0;
		display: grid;
		gap: 0.4rem;
	}
	.release-list li {
		display: flex;
		gap: 0.75rem;
		align-items: baseline;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
	}
	.release-date,
	.release-count {
		color: var(--sl-color-gray-3);
		font-size: var(--sl-text-sm);
	}
	.home {
		--gap: clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 3rem);
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
		gap: calc(var(--gap) * 1.25);
		margin-top: 0.5rem;
	}
	.home > *,
	.intro > * {
		min-width: 0;
	}
	.home h2 {
		font-size: var(--sl-text-xl);
		font-weight: 650;
		letter-spacing: -0.01em;
		color: var(--sl-color-white);
		margin: 0 0 1rem;
	}
	.home a {
		color: var(--sl-color-text-accent);
		text-underline-offset: 0.2em;
		text-decoration-thickness: 1px;
	}

	.intro {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.8fr) minmax(0, 1.2fr);
		gap: var(--gap);
		align-items: start;
	}
	.lead p {
		color: var(--sl-color-gray-2);
		line-height: 1.65;
		margin: 0 0 1rem;
		max-width: 38rem;
	}
	.lead .summary {
		font-size: var(--sl-text-xl);
		line-height: 1.5;
		color: var(--sl-color-white);
		text-wrap: pretty;
	}
	.actions {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		gap: 0.6rem;
		margin: 1.5rem 0 1.25rem;
	}
	.button {
		display: inline-flex;
		align-items: center;
		padding: 0.5rem 0.95rem;
		border: 1px solid var(--sl-color-gray-5);
		border-radius: 6px;
		font-size: var(--sl-text-sm);
		font-weight: 600;
		color: var(--sl-color-white) !important;
		text-decoration: none;
		transition: border-color 160ms cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1), background-color 160ms cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1);
	}
	.button:hover {
		border-color: var(--sl-color-gray-3);
		background: var(--sl-color-gray-6);
	}
	.button.primary {
		background: var(--sl-color-accent);
		border-color: var(--sl-color-accent);
		color: var(--sl-color-black) !important;
	}
	.button.primary:hover {
		background: var(--sl-color-accent-high);
		border-color: var(--sl-color-accent-high);
	}
	.lead .meta {
		font-size: var(--sl-text-sm);
		color: var(--sl-color-gray-3);
	}
	.shot :global(.screenshot) {
		margin: 0;
	}

	.why ul {
		margin: 0 0 1rem;
		padding: 0;
		list-style: none;
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
		gap: 1rem 1.5rem;
	}
	.why li {
		color: var(--sl-color-gray-2);
		line-height: 1.6;
	}
	.why strong {
		color: var(--sl-color-white);
		font-weight: 650;
	}
	.why p {
		margin: 0;
	}
	.covers-lead {
		color: var(--sl-color-gray-2);
		margin: 0 0 1rem;
	}
	.covers dl {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
		gap: 0 1.5rem;
		margin: 0;
		border-top: 1px solid var(--sl-color-hairline-light);
	}
	.covers dl > div {
		padding: 1rem 0 1.1rem;
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--sl-color-hairline);
	}
	.covers dt {
		font-weight: 650;
		margin-bottom: 0.3rem;
	}
	.covers dt a {
		color: var(--sl-color-white);
		text-decoration: none;
	}
	.covers dt a:hover {
		color: var(--sl-color-text-accent);
		text-decoration: underline;
	}
	.covers dd {
		margin: 0;
		color: var(--sl-color-gray-2);
		font-size: var(--sl-text-sm);
		line-height: 1.55;
	}

	.flow-grid {
		display: grid;
		gap: 1rem;
	}
	.diagram {
		margin: 0;
		padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
		border: 1px solid var(--sl-color-hairline-light);
		border-radius: 6px;
		background: var(--sl-color-gray-6);
		color: var(--sl-color-gray-1);
		font-family: var(--__sl-font-mono);
		font-size: 0.8rem;
		line-height: 1.5;
		overflow-x: auto;
	}
	.flow p,
	.phone p,
	.install p,
	.next li {
		color: var(--sl-color-gray-2);
	}
	.flow p {
		margin: 0;
		line-height: 1.65;
		max-width: 50rem;
	}

	.phone {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
		gap: var(--gap);
		align-items: start;
		/* The heading's rule, across both columns. */
		border-top: 1px solid var(--sl-color-hairline);
		padding-top: 1.25rem;
	}
	.phone h2 {
		border-top: none;
		padding-top: 0;
	}
	.phone-text p {
		margin: 0 0 1rem;
		max-width: 34rem;
	}
	.phones {
		display: flex;
		gap: 1rem;
	}
	.phones :global(.screenshot) {
		margin: 0;
		width: 15rem;
	}

	.install p {
		margin: 0 0 1rem;
	}
	.install .after {
		margin-top: 1rem;
		font-size: var(--sl-text-sm);
	}
	.install code {
		font-family: var(--__sl-font-mono);
		font-size: 0.86em;
		color: var(--bc-code-text);
		background: var(--sl-color-bg-inline-code);
		border: 1px solid var(--sl-color-hairline);
		border-radius: 4px;
		padding: 0.08em 0.32em;
	}

	.changes-head {
		display: flex;
		align-items: baseline;
		justify-content: space-between;
		gap: 1rem;
		border-top: 1px solid var(--sl-color-hairline);
		padding-top: 1.25rem;
		margin-bottom: 1rem;
	}
	.changes-head h2 {
		margin: 0;
	}
	.changes-head a {
		font-size: var(--sl-text-sm);
	}

	.next ol {
		margin: 0;
		padding-left: 1.25rem;
		display: grid;
		gap: 0.35rem;
	}

	@media (max-width: 64rem) {
		.intro {
			grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
		}
		.covers dl {
			grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
		}
		.phone {
			grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
		}
	}
	@media (max-width: 40rem) {
		.why ul {
			grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
		}
		.covers dl {
			grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
		}
		.lead .summary {
			font-size: var(--sl-text-lg);
		}
		.phones :global(.screenshot) {
			width: auto;
			flex: 1 1 0;
			min-width: 0;
		}
	}
</style>
